No—under those conditions, the Moon would be effectively impossible to see with normal unaided eyesight.

Although geometrically above the horizon, it is:

- only **0.09% illuminated**, producing an extraordinarily thin crescent;
- just **3.445° from the Sun**, deep in the Sun’s glare;
- only **10.5° above the horizon**, where atmospheric extinction and scattering are substantial;
- in **bright daylight**, with the Sun already about **13.7° high**.

The crescent’s illuminated width would be only about **0.3 arcsecond**, far below naked-eye resolution, and its contrast against the nearby bright sky would be negligible. Even expert observers generally cannot detect such a young Moon unaided at so small a solar elongation. Optical searching would also be hazardous because of the Moon’s proximity to the Sun.

**Practical verdict: not visible to the unaided eye, despite being above the horizon.**

